{
  "dom_id": "step-q163797",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033857/assets/f0ca128a0755c0419d03d119501210b4be8036ccc0148f21b0e3a3665def30d5.png)\n\nTwo point masses $A$ and $B$ are connected by a rigid, massless rod of length $10\\,m$ that is perfectly balanced on a fulcrum, as shown above. If $B$ has mass $3\\,kg$ and is $4\\,m$ from the fulcrum, find the mass of $A.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let $m$ be the mass of $A.$\nThe length of the rod is $10\\,m$ and the distance between the fulcrum and $B$ is $4\\,m.$ So, the distance between the fulcrum and $A$ is\n\n$$\n10-4=6\\,m,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033857/assets/a68789963345d21ca403d22a5f97ee440b0c3baa36345b0161bc7f80a8e0d1e0.png)\n\nLet's write down the moment of each mass about the fulcrum:\n\n- The moment of mass $A$ equals $-m\\cdot 6=-6m\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}$ (negative since the motion induced by this mass would be counterclockwise).\n\n- The moment of mass $B$ equals $3\\cdot 4=12\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}$ (positive since the motion induced by this mass would be clockwise).\n\nThe *principle of moments* tells us that for the two masses to balance perfectly, the sum of the moments must equal zero.\n\nTherefore, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-6m & =0 \\\\ 6m & =12 \\\\ m & =2\\,kg.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
